---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/advanced/conflict-and-peace-studies">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the empirical flagship of the political-science tier. It assumes the institutional vocabulary of
    <em>Forms of Government</em>, the analytical lenses of <em>International Relations: Theories</em>, and the
    regime-and-institutions ground of <em>Constitutions and Rights</em> — civil wars, after all, are fights over
    exactly those institutions.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>reproduce the bargaining-range example and explain why any deal inside it beats fighting;</li>
    <li>name Fearon's three bargaining blocks and match a real standoff to each;</li>
    <li>summarise the durable civil-war risk factors, and say why recidivism, not onset, is the policy leverage;</li>
    <li>state what Fortna's peacekeeping evidence shows and the credible-commitment mechanism behind it;</li>
    <li>distinguish negative from positive peace, and explain why peacebuilding is slower and harder than peacemaking.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    War looks like passion, and peace like luck. Conflict studies built the counter-frame:
    organised violence is a <strong>strategic phenomenon, measurable and modelable</strong> — and
    its foundational result is almost paradoxical. War is costly; costly contests should be
    replaced by bargained settlements that both sides prefer; therefore <strong>every war is a
    bargaining failure</strong>, and the science of war becomes the science of what blocks deals.
    Everything in this chapter — interstate war, civil war, terrorism, peacekeeping,
    peacebuilding — runs through that reframing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bargaining framework, civil-war risk factors, what peacekeeping does, peacebuilding's honest record" widget="equationStep" config={conflictCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are the field in miniature — the bargaining framework that
    reframes war, the civil-war risk factors that survived testing, the peacekeeping result that answered a
    cynical debate with data, and peacebuilding's honest record. Watch the through-line: each card converts a
    moralised question ("is war irrational?", "does the UN work?") into a measurable one — the advanced
    method this lesson teaches, beyond any single finding.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> war is costly, so a deal both sides prefer should always exist — the field's
    foundational puzzle. The next section runs the numbers and then asks what blocks the deal in practice.
  </p>

  <h2>The bargaining theory of war</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> Two states contest a prize worth 100. Side A would win a war
    with probability p = 0.6; war costs A c<sub>1</sub> = 20 and B c<sub>2</sub> = 25. A's
    expected value of fighting is 0.6 × 100 − 20 = 40; B's is 0.4 × 100 − 25 = 15. Total value
    destroyed by fighting: 45. Any deal giving A an x between 40 and 75 leaves both better off
    than war (A gets more than 40, B keeps more than 15). The <strong>bargaining range</strong>
    is 35 units wide — and rational actors should find it. Fearon's answer (1995) explains why
    they still fight, with three mechanisms. <strong>Private information and incentives to
    misrepresent:</strong> each side exaggerates its strength and resolve to bargain harder, and
    the exaggeration is credible only if backed by a willingness that cannot be demonstrated
    short of fighting — war as the costly signal. <strong>Commitment problems:</strong> even a
    good deal today cannot bind tomorrow — if power is shifting (a rising side, a
    demobilising army, an oil discovery), the currently strong side may prefer war now to a
    deal it will regret; the mechanism explains why <em>power transitions</em> are the classic
    danger zone. <strong>Indivisibility:</strong> genuinely unsplittable stakes (a holy city, a
    throne) narrow the range — though on inspection most "indivisible" stakes turn out to be
    side payments away from divisible, leaving the first two mechanisms to do most of the work.
    The theory's power is diagnostic: it converted "why war?" from grand theorising into a
    checklist of testable bargaining blocks.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each bargaining block with its signature" widget="match" config={confFearonMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "wars happen because leaders are irrational or driven by pure
    passion." The rationalist frame does not deny emotion or error — it shows that
    <em>fully rational</em> actors with complete information and credible commitments would never
    fight, so real wars are traceable to specific, remediable failures: misinformation,
    unenforceable promises, institutions that fail to signal. Even the rationalist frictions
    themselves are institutional facts (audience costs, transparency, alliance guarantees are all
    information-and-commitment machinery).
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — the rationalist baseline" widget="cloze" config={confRationalCloze} />

  <h2>Interstate war: transitions, regimes, and the data revolution</h2>
  <p>
    The interstate findings that survive scrutiny: <strong>power transition beats power
    parity</strong> as a predictor — wars cluster when a rising challenger approaches a ruling
    hegemon and commitment problems peak (Organski's power-transition theory; the "Thucydides
    trap" is its ancient branding, though careful histories note the rhetorical trap overstates
    the inevitability — roughly half of such transitions have avoided war). The
    <strong>democratic peace</strong> is among political science's most robust empirical
    generalisations: consolidated democracies almost never fight each other (dyadic claim),
    though they fight autocracies at normal rates. Its refinement matters more than its
    celebration: <strong>democratisation transitions are dangerous</strong> — Mansfield and
    Snyder's finding that partial, turbulent democratisations fight <em>more</em>, because
    mobilised publics, weak courts and nationalist incentives combine before institutions
    consolidate. Underneath both stands the <strong>data revolution</strong>: the Correlates of
    War project (Singer, from 1963) and the UCDP/PRIO battle-death data converted war from essay
    topic to measurable series — enabling the trend debates (has organised violence declined?
    Pinker's case versus critics who dispute the baseline periods and the weighting of
    great-power wars) that keep the field honest.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — the democratic peace and its neighbours" widget="judge" config={confDemoJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "democracies never fight, so spreading democracy ends war." The finding is
  narrower and its refinement sharper: consolidated democracies almost never fight <em>each other</em> (the
  dyadic claim), but they fight autocracies at normal rates — and the transition is the danger. Mansfield and
    Snyder show partial, turbulent democratisations fight <em>more</em>, because mobilised publics, weak courts
  and nationalist incentives combine before institutions consolidate. The correct picture: the democratic peace
  is a property of dyads of consolidated democracies, and the road there passes through the weakest institutions
  a state will ever have.</p>

  <h2>Civil war: the risk-factor revolution</h2>
  <p>
    Civil wars became the dominant form of organised violence after 1945, and their study was
    transformed by Collier and Hoeffler's <strong>greed versus grievance</strong> challenge:
    proxies for opportunity (lootable resources — gemstones, coca, oil; low income; small and
    dispersed populations; mountainous terrain) predicted onset better than proxies for
    grievance (inequality, repression, ethnic diversity alone). The strong claim did not fully
    survive — grievance variables (political exclusion of ethnic groups, Cederman and
    colleagues' work) matter once measured properly, and the greed-grievance dichotomy is false
    (rebellion needs both motive and opportunity; fearon's cleaner formulation: insurgency
    requires <em>where</em> and <em>what to fight over</em>). The durable findings: low income is
    the strongest single risk factor (poor states fight themselves more); oil dependence raises
    risk (rents finance rebels and corrupt the state); <strong>previous civil war is the best
    predictor of the next</strong> — recidivism, not onset, is where the policy leverage is; and
    diaspora financing, cold-war patronage and neighbourhood diffusion shape incidence. Ethnic
    diversity per se does not predict war — polarisation between a few large groups with
    exclusionary politics does, a distinction with real policy consequences.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each factor under its civil-war verdict" widget="sort-buckets" config={confRiskSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>Terrorism</strong> fits the same strategic frame: Crenshaw's insight — terrorism is
    <strong>communication by the weak</strong>, violence aimed less at victims than at audiences
    (governments to provoke overreaction, publics to terrify or inspire, recruits to mobilise) —
    explains target selection (symbolism, timing, media logic) better than irrationality models,
    and it has operational teeth: counter-terrorism that plays the adversary's game (massive
    repression that validates grievance narratives) can strengthen recruitment — the empirical
    pattern behind the "war on terror" after-action debates.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="walk the counter-terrorism decision from the strategic frame" widget="branch" config={confTerrorBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the risk-factor revolution found what predicts civil war — income, oil, prior
    conflict, exclusion — and terrorism fits the same frame. Next: the peace side — what actually keeps wars
    from restarting.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually ends wars and keeps them ended</h2>
  <p>
    Galtung's distinction organises the peace side: <strong>negative peace</strong> (fighting
    stopped) versus <strong>positive peace</strong> (the structural conditions generating violence
    dismantled). The empirical answers, in increasing difficulty. <strong>Victory</strong> ends
    wars durably but rarely produces legitimate order. <strong>Negotiated settlements</strong>
    fail often because they solve information without solving commitment (the demobilised army
    problem) — which is exactly where <strong>peacekeeping enters</strong>: Fortna's matched
    comparisons showed deployments cut recurrence risk roughly in half, not by defeating anyone
    but by making promises enforceable — monitors, verification, and the cost imposed on
    cheaters; every additional instrument (size, mandate, consent of parties) adds measurable
    protection. <strong>Peacebuilding</strong> is the slow layer: power-sharing designs
    (consociationalism — Lijphart's elite cartel, proportional representation, group autonomy;
    adopted in Bosnia, Northern Ireland, Lebanon — stabilises while entrenching the identities
    it moderates, the honest trade), <strong>DDR</strong> (disarmament, demobilisation,
    reintegration — where budgets go to die; reintegration failure predicts re-recruitment),
    <strong>transitional justice</strong> (trials, truth commissions, amnesties — the
    peace-versus-justice dilemma with no clean answer: victims' recognition versus elites'
    incentives to fight on), and state-building whose record depends on whether institutions
    distribute power or merely refinance it. The measured pattern is encouraging at the margin:
    civil wars that end today stay ended longer than in previous decades — peace maintenance is
    one of the era's documented policy successes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="order the peace toolkit from ending fighting to building peace" widget="order" config={confPeaceOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "peacekeepers stop wars by fighting, or by deterring the stronger side." The
  evidence shows neither: Fortna's matched comparisons locate the mechanism in <em>credible commitment</em> —
  monitors, verification and the cost imposed on cheaters make both sides' promises enforceable, cutting
  recurrence risk roughly in half without defeating anyone. It seems reasonable to picture blue helmets as
  soldiers; the correct picture is closer to referees and escrow agents — which is why every additional
  instrument, from size to mandate to consent, adds measurable protection.</p>

  <h2>The risk-factor model of conflict onset</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative turn produced a risk architecture worth knowing precisely. Interstate
    war's predictors: contiguity (neighbours fight), power transition (above), territorial
    disputes, and regime combinations. Civil war's: low income (the strongest single factor —
    poor states fight themselves), slow growth, oil dependence (rents finance rebellion and
    corrupt governance), mountainous terrain (insurgency's classic geography), large diasporas
    (financing from abroad), prior conflict (recidivism dominates the risk landscape), and
    ethnic <strong>exclusion</strong> — where the refined measurement matters: diversity per
    se does not predict war; politically excluded, mobilised groups do (the Cederman-Wimmer
    rehabilitation of grievance with better data). The policy translation is the conflict
    early-warning programmes: risk models flag countries for preventive diplomacy — with the
    honest record that prevention is hard to prove (success is invisible: the war that did
    not happen) and that the models flag while humans decide. The recurring finding across
    all levels: <strong>conflict is recurrent</strong> — around half of civil-war countries
    relapse within a decade — which reframes the whole field from onset to maintenance of
    peace.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet the magnitude — relapse within a decade" widget="estimate" config={confRelapseEstimate} />

  <h2>Coercion: sanctions, bombing and their honest record</h2>
  <p>
    Between diplomacy and war sits coercion, and its record is the field's most
    policy-relevant finding. <strong>Economic sanctions</strong>: imposed hundreds of times
    since 1914, with success rates (target concedes) around a third in the classic Hufbauer
    dataset, lower in re-analyses correcting for selection (sanctions imposed where pressure
    was already likely to work); multilateral outperform unilateral; democracies and allies
    concede more; and the humanitarian costs — the Iraq-1990s case changed the field — pushed
    design toward targeted or "smart" sanctions (asset freezes on elites, arms embargoes)
    instead of comprehensive embargoes. <strong>Air power and punishment strategies</strong>:
    the empirical record (Second World War strategic bombing, Vietnam's Rolling Thunder, the
    Gulf air campaign) shows punishment rarely breaks civilian morale, and that coercion
    succeeds when demands are limited, the threat credible and incremental — Pape's
    denial-versus-punishment distinction being the durable framework. The bargaining theory's
    translation: coercion is bargaining with violence as the currency, and it fails for
    exactly Fearon's reasons — signals are cheap to misread, commitment is scarce, and the
    target's information problems are the coercer's problem too.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="one line of this coercion briefing is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={confCoerceSpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In the worked example, fighting leaves A with 40 and B with 15 of the 100-unit prize. Explain why that
    arithmetic guarantees some negotiated split beats war for both — and what the destroyed 45 represents.</li>
    <li>An oil discovery will shift power toward a rising state over the next decade. Which of Fearon's
    mechanisms makes war more likely <em>now</em> rather than later, and why?</li>
    <li>Why is a raw comparison of wars "with versus without peacekeepers" the wrong way to measure
    peacekeeping, and what did Fortna's design measure instead?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Fighting burns 45 units in costs, leaving 55 of the prize's value intact.
    Any deal giving each side more than its war value — A more than 40, B more than 15 — divides the prize
    without paying the costs, so both prefer it. The destroyed 45 is the surplus rational actors leave on the
    table: every war is a bargaining failure to be explained, not an equilibrium to be assumed. (2) A commitment problem. A deal struck today cannot bind tomorrow's stronger A
    to its terms, so the currently strong side may prefer fighting while it still can — power transitions, not
    power parity, are the classic danger zone. (3) Because peacekeepers are not sent at random: they deploy into
    the hardest (or easiest) cases, so raw comparisons mislead through selection. Fortna matched post-war cases
    on the confounders and measured the risk of war resuming — roughly halved, through credible commitment
    rather than combat.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>War is costly, so a bargained settlement both sides prefer always exists — every war is a bargaining failure to be explained.</li>
    <li>Fearon's three blocks: private information and incentives to misrepresent, commitment problems, rarely indivisible stakes.</li>
    <li>Power transitions, not parity, cluster interstate war; partial democratisations fight more than stable autocracies.</li>
    <li>Civil war's durable risks: low income, oil dependence, mountainous terrain, diasporas — and above all, previous civil war.</li>
    <li>Ethnic diversity per se does not predict war; politically excluded, mobilised groups do.</li>
    <li>Peacekeeping roughly halves recurrence risk by making promises enforceable — credible commitment, not combat.</li>
    <li>Negative peace stops fighting; positive peace dismantles the structures that generate it — the slower, harder job.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Political Economy</em> picks up the resources-and-institutions thread this lesson kept touching — oil
    rents, sanctions, the economics of post-war recovery. <em>Comparative Politics Methods</em> trains the
    matching and measurement logic on which the field's strongest findings here depend.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Formal extensions.</strong> Powell's commitment-problem theorems unified the field:
    preventive war logic (attack now because relative power will deteriorate), the
    demobilisation dilemma, and resource-curse dynamics all fall out of one model — the
    rationalist programme's unified theory moment. <strong>Measurement frontiers.</strong> UCDP's
    25-battle-death threshold, licensing precision and arbitrariness; conflict-event datasets
    (ACLED) now geocode violence daily; machine analysis of news and satellite imagery (night
    lights as economic activity in war zones) extends measurement where surveys cannot go —
    each advance reopening baseline disputes. <strong>Causality fights.</strong> Does foreign aid
    prolong conflict? Do interventions select into the hardest cases (the selection problem that
    made early peacekeeping studies flattering and later matched studies sobering)? The field's
    causal-inference standards were sharpened precisely by these controversies.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> the climate-conflict link (temperature anomalies raise
    intergroup conflict risk in many settings — magnitude, mechanism and policy translation
    remain contested); cyber operations as coercive bargaining (cheap, deniable — does the
    bargaining framework survive when signalling is deniable by design?); autonomous weapons and
    the commitment machinery of deterrence; urbanisation of conflict (cities as the new terrain);
    and the stabilisation-entrenchment trade in power-sharing — can institutional design
    transcend the identities it must acknowledge to end a war? The century's peace architecture
    is being argued, not inherited.
  </p>
  <p>
    Post-conflict economics deserves its own ledger, because it is where peace is actually
    built. The empirics: economies rebound after wars end (growth resumes as capital
    rebuilds), but the rebound's distribution determines durability — exclusion from
    recovery re-arms grievances, and youth unemployment in post-war settings is among the
    strongest recidivism predictors (the demobilised-fighter employment problem). Aid's
    record in post-conflict settings is genuinely mixed: capacity constraints, capture, and
    the resource-dependence trap recur. The institutional layer: transitional justice's
    record (trials' deterrence effects modest and contested; truth commissions'
    contributions to acknowledgment and reform real but slow), security-sector reform
    (whose soldiers disband is often the settlement's real test), and the durable political
    settlement — elite bargains that make violence unnecessary — as the variable separating
    the peace that holds from the pause between wars. The synthesis: peace is not the
    absence of war economics but a different economics — Galtung's positive peace, rendered
    in budgets.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the gendered dimension, long under-measured, is now central: conflict's costs and
    peacebuilding's outcomes differ sharply by gender (wartime sexual violence as strategy and
    crime; women's participation in peace processes correlating with agreement durability in
    the cross-national record), and the WPS agenda (the UN's Women, Peace and Security
    resolutions) institutionalised the finding — implementation uneven, the evidence base
    solid.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: the bargaining range, transitions, peacekeeping, peace types" widget="quiz" config={confQuiz} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each item maps to a section above — the costly-war result, the
    democratisation danger zone, what Fortna actually measured, and Galtung's two peaces. Use the feedback to
    separate neighbouring claims: possibility demonstrated versus explained, a peacekept settlement versus a
    self-sustaining peace, absence of fighting versus absence of the conditions that generate it.
  </p>

